── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #630  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Clarify that tpush writes both major identity (a0) and minor identity (a1) for external interrupts, preventing handler mis-dispatch when multiple interrupt controllers share overlapping minor interrupt numbers
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-intc.c:29-35  riscv_intc_irq
      Dispatches on regs->cause (scause) minor identity alone, stripped of CAUSE_IRQ_FLAG. This is the convergence point for all non-AIA external interrupts. The implicit invariant is that a single interrupt controller (e.g., PLIC) is the sole source of external interrupts on this hart. Under Smtp, multiple controllers could inject through the same scause=IRQ_S_EXT, and the minor identity alone cannot disambiguate which controller originated the interrupt — colliding hwirqs from different controllers would be routed to the wrong handler.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-intc.c:37-43  riscv_intc_aia_irq
      Reads CSR_TOPI and extracts only TOPI_IID_SHIFT (minor identity) for dispatch. The TOPI CSR in the current AIA spec encodes IID (bits 27:16) and IPRIO (bits 7:0) — no major identity field exists. Under Smtp, if TOPI is extended with a major-identity field (e.g., identifying which IMSIC domain), this code silently discards it. All AIA-mode interrupts from potentially multiple controllers converge here with only the local interrupt ID.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:125-238  handle_exception
      Trap entry saves x1-x31 (including a0/a1 where Smtp would place major/minor identity) into pt_regs, then overwrites a0 with the pt_regs pointer before calling do_irq. The saved a0/a1 values are preserved in pt_regs but never consulted for interrupt dispatch. The IRQ-vs-exception branch is taken on scause (s4) alone at line 218 (bge s4, zero, 1f). If Smtp hardware injects major identity into a0/a1 via tpush, the kernel entry path preserves the values structurally but the dispatch chain never reads them. The saved a0/a1 would need to be extracted from pt_regs and forwarded through the IRQ handling chain.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/traps.c:438-459  handle_riscv_irq / do_irq
      The C-level trap-to-IRQ bridge: do_irq → handle_riscv_irq → handle_arch_irq(regs). pt_regs carries the saved a0/a1 (from entry.S) through this chain, but neither function extracts or forwards a major identity. handle_arch_irq is a function pointer (set by set_handle_irq) that receives only pt_regs — there is no out-of-band channel for major identity. The entire IRQ dispatch architecture assumes that the interrupt controller identity is implicit in which handle_arch_irq was registered, not explicit in the trap frame.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-riscv-imsic-early.c:93-125  imsic_handle_irq
